Let’s split the sum over subsets as:

$$
\text{Number of odd-sized subsets} = \sum_{\substack{k \text{ odd}}} \binom{n}{k}
$$

We want to **count** this sum.

We can use the following idea:

Consider the following two identities (from the binomial theorem):

$$
(1 + 1)^n = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k}
\quad \text{and} \quad
(1 - 1)^n = \sum_{k=0}^{n} (-1)^k \binom{n}{k}
$$

Now take the **difference** of the two:

$$
(1 + 1)^n - (1 - 1)^n = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \binom{n}{k} - \sum_{k=0}^{n} (-1)^k \binom{n}{k}
$$

